[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRRouting (bgpd) on router1. Mechanism: With bgpd down on router1, the eBGP session to router2 never forms, so neither router installs a route to the other’s LAN. Traffic from pc1 (195.11.14.2/24) destined for pc2 (200.1.1.2/24) reaches router1 but is dropped there due to lack of a route to 200.1.1.0/24.

Localization and evidence:
- router1: frr.service is failed (systemctl shows “Active: failed”), and bgpd is not running. Its routing table has only connected routes (193.10.11.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24), no route to 200.1.1.0/24. 
- router2: FRR is running. “show ip bgp summary” shows neighbor 193.10.11.1 in Active state with no prefixes received, consistent with the peer being down. Its routing table has only connected networks (193.10.11.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24).
- The inter-router link (193.10.11.0/24) and host-facing interfaces are up on both routers, so the dataplane is fine; only control-plane BGP on router1 is down.
- router1’s /etc/frr/frr.conf contains correct BGP config (AS 1, neighbor 193.10.11.2 remote-as 2, network 195.11.14.0/24), so a service restart should restore peering.

Root cause: FRRouting service on router1 stopped/failed, causing loss of eBGP reachability. 

Corrective fix: Restart FRR on router1 (systemctl restart frr) and ensure it’s enabled. Verify “show ip bgp summary” forms Established and both routers learn 195.11.14.0/24 and 200.1.1.0/24. No restart is required on router2.
=== END ===